                <pb facs="0442" n="324" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg324"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.124A/57: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Liberia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wharton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Monrovia</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-10-02T09:00:00-00:44">October 2, 1929—9
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 3—11:30 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>53. Referring to my telegram No. 52, September 24, 12 p.m. No further cases, Dr.
                    Walcott left yesterday for Lagos. His report to be submitted in the near
                    future.</p>
                <p>He believes yellow fever has existed here since 1925, and will recommend health
                    officer.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wharton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
